  "label": "fact",
  "about": "Airline aircraft OpenSky Network's receivers saw in two small boxes (Lisbon, Madeira), 2026-11-11 22:00 to :59 UTC, one snapshot every 10 minutes (Lisbon) or 20 minutes (Madeira). Airline flights only (an airline's ICAO code plus a flight number); every other aircraft is dropped and only counted. Fact: what OpenSky shows at the time read; positions can be up to a few seconds old (time_position). Not the storm's peak: anonymous OpenSky has no history, so these start on 11 November at about 22:15 UTC.",
  "not_a_warning_service": "Not a warning service. No forecast or advice of ours. Your airline decides whether your flight runs: its site or app has the latest. For warnings follow IPMA and ANEPC.",
  "attribution": "The OpenSky Network, https://opensky-network.org (M. Schäfer, M. Strohmeier, V. Lenders, I. Martinovic, M. Wilhelm, \"Bringing Up OpenSky: A Large-scale ADS-B Sensor Network for Research\", IPSN 2014). Used for research and non-commercial purposes, as its API documentation asks; its full terms page is still for a person to read (transport/sources.md).",
  "units": {
    "baro_alt_m": "metres, barometric",
